# {{PROJECT_NAME}} - Claude Code notes

`AGENTS.md`, imported on the first line above, is the single source of this project's
identity, brain backend, and hard rules. Claude Code expands that import at the start of
every session, so the rules are already in context by the time you read this.

Do not copy any rule from `AGENTS.md` into this file. A second copy of the rules is
exactly the parallel-versions failure this project exists to prevent, and two files that
must be kept "identical" by hand always drift. Everything below is Claude Code specific
and has no equivalent for other agents.

## Session ritual

Start of session: read the re-entry page named in `AGENTS.md` before doing real work,
and say the staleness signal out loud (when the brain was last updated, and by whom).

End of session, or as soon as a unit of work is finished: run `/project-checkpoint`.
Do not leave the write-back to the next session; that is how the brain goes stale.

## Before you say "done"

1. Confirm the change matches what the user asked for.
2. Confirm the brain was updated if a unit of work was completed, or state why no
   update was needed.
3. Report briefly: what changed, where in the brain it was logged, what to verify.

## Auto memory is off for this project

`.claude/settings.json` sets `"autoMemoryEnabled": false`. Auto memory is on by default
and would accumulate project notes in `~/.claude/projects/<project>/memory/`, which is
machine-local and invisible to teammates: a second store of project state that no one
else can read and that no checkpoint updates. The brain named in `AGENTS.md` is the only
place project state belongs.

If you catch yourself wanting to save a project fact "for next time", that fact belongs
in the brain, not in memory.
